00:15:13,060 --> 00:15:15,060
I feel like I'm forgetting something.

276
00:15:16,060 --> 00:15:17,060
About the screening?

277
00:15:19,060 --> 00:15:21,060
Or about the audition yesterday?

278
00:15:21,060 --> 00:15:22,060
That's it?

279
00:15:22,060 --> 00:15:23,060
Pompa remembers what it is.

280
00:15:23,060 --> 00:15:25,060
A little light bulb went on.

281
00:15:25,060 --> 00:15:27,060
I didn't notice at the time.

282
00:15:27,060 --> 00:15:28,060
Here it is.

283
00:15:28,060 --> 00:15:29,060
Arrange a meeting with her.

284
00:15:29,060 --> 00:15:31,060
Uh, yes, ma'am.

285
00:15:33,060 --> 00:15:35,060
Uh, this girl.

286
00:15:35,060 --> 00:15:38,060
Does she have the eyes of an outcast, too?

287
00:15:38,060 --> 00:15:39,060
No!

288
00:15:39,060 --> 00:15:41,060
Because an actress needs sparkling eyes!

289
00:15:41,060 --> 00:15:43,060
I can't put my finger on it yet.

290
00:15:43,060 --> 00:15:46,060
But there's just something about her.

291
00:15:46,060 --> 00:15:47,060
No matter.

292
00:15:47,060 --> 00:15:49,060
Because when it comes to making movies,

293
00:15:49,060 --> 00:15:52,060
sometimes you just gotta go with your gut.

294
00:15:52,060 --> 00:15:53,060
Right?

295
00:15:53,060 --> 00:16:03,060
Um, would it be okay if I use the screening room?

296
00:16:03,060 --> 00:16:04,060
Sure!

297
00:16:04,060 --> 00:16:05,060
Whatcha gonna watch?

298
00:16:05,060 --> 00:16:08,060
Just, uh, cinema paradiso.

299
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:10,060
Ugh, I hate that one.

300
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:11,060
What?

301
00:16:11,060 --> 00:16:13,060
But that movie's a masterpiece.

302
00:16:13,060 --> 00:16:16,060
Eh, it's a good story, but it's way too long.

303
00:16:16,060 --> 00:16:18,060
So, you don't like long movies?

304
00:16:18,060 --> 00:16:19,060
Ugh, I hate them.

305
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:23,060
But don't you think the longer they are, the longer you have to enjoy them?

306
00:16:23,060 --> 00:16:24,060
You got it all wrong.

307
00:16:24,060 --> 00:16:26,060
Let me tell you a story.

308
00:16:28,060 --> 00:16:32,060
When I was a kid, my parents were always busy.

309
00:16:32,060 --> 00:16:34,060
So, my grandpa raised me.

310
00:16:34,060 --> 00:16:38,060
And the only thing my grandpa knows are movies.

311
00:16:38,060 --> 00:16:40,060
So he made me watch them.

312
00:16:40,060 --> 00:16:45,060
Do you know how tough it is for a little kid to sit still for two to three hours?

313
00:16:45,060 --> 00:16:51,060
He would force me to watch the entire thing and then make me tell him what I thought.

314
00:16:51,060 --> 00:16:58,060
So a simple story told in 90 minutes or less was like an oasis in a desert of celluloid.

315
00:17:00,060 --> 00:17:04,060
And that is one of the reasons why I can't stand long movies.

316
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:06,060
What are the others?

317
00:17:06,060 --> 00:17:12,060
Making people concentrate for two hours or sometimes more is simply unkind to modern audiences.

318
00:17:13,060 --> 00:17:19,060
Filmmakers should recognize that every shot is precious and attempt to convey their vision as concisely as possible.

319
00:17:19,060 --> 00:17:21,060
I mean, a bloated film isn't beautiful.

320
00:17:21,060 --> 00:17:23,060
It's just indulgent, yeah?

321
00:17:23,060 --> 00:17:29,060
But I love watching movies so much that I don't mind sitting for three or even four hours if...

322
00:17:29,060 --> 00:17:32,060
Ah! This is what's wrong with you film buffs!

323
00:17:32,060 --> 00:17:38,060
Being able to pick the brain of one of the industry's greats from such a young age...

324
00:17:39,060 --> 00:17:41,060
I envy her.

325
00:17:41,060 --> 00:17:42,060
Mind if I join you?

326
00:17:42,060 --> 00:17:43,060
Huh?

327
00:17:44,060 --> 00:17:45,060
Miss Pompoh!

328
00:17:45,060 --> 00:17:47,060
Did you want to watch the movie as well?

329
00:17:47,060 --> 00:17:49,060
It's already halfway over though.

330
00:17:49,060 --> 00:17:50,060
Great!

331
00:17:50,060 --> 00:17:52,060
Cause I didn't want to watch the whole thing.

332
00:17:53,060 --> 00:17:57,060
If it's a masterpiece, then checking it out is only apropos.

333
00:17:57,060 --> 00:17:58,060
Ugh.

334
00:18:13,060 --> 00:18:14,060
Huh?

335
00:18:14,060 --> 00:18:16,060
I'm getting deja vu.

336
00:18:20,060 --> 00:18:22,060
I wonder why...

337
00:18:24,060 --> 00:18:25,060
Good morning, Miss Pompoh!

338
00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:26,040
Huh?

339
00:18:28,660 --> 00:18:30,800
I guess she hasn't come in yet.

340
00:18:38,480 --> 00:18:41,700
Meister, is this what Pompo's been working on?

341
00:18:55,060 --> 00:19:09,560
Pompo has returned for breakfast!

342
00:19:10,220 --> 00:19:11,860
Probably shouldn't have eaten so much,

343
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:13,680
because this is one stuffed tummy.

344
00:19:14,120 --> 00:19:14,960
Okay, noted.

345
00:19:15,180 --> 00:19:16,120
How do you like the script?

346
00:19:16,640 --> 00:19:17,300
Oh, well, I...

347
00:19:17,300 --> 00:19:18,800
I agree. It's so true.

348
00:19:19,120 --> 00:19:21,720
Pompo is simply incapable of writing a boring script.

349
00:19:21,720 --> 00:19:24,620
I want you to give me a detailed analysis right now.

350
00:19:25,620 --> 00:19:27,620
Yes, I... I can do that.

351
00:19:28,580 --> 00:19:30,340
An aging artist finds peace

352
00:19:30,340 --> 00:19:32,900
when he travels to the countryside and meets a young girl.

353
00:19:33,300 --> 00:19:35,000
This story's a bit of a cliché.

354
00:19:35,620 --> 00:19:36,240
But that said,

355
00:19:36,640 --> 00:19:38,980
these characters are completely unique and compelling.

356
00:19:40,060 --> 00:19:42,540
Delbert, the arrogant and talented conductor

357
00:19:42,540 --> 00:19:43,800
yet naive about the world.

358
00:19:44,320 --> 00:19:46,960
Lily, who at times acts like an inquisitive boy

359
00:19:46,960 --> 00:19:48,960
and at others, like a sensitive girl.

360
00:19:49,220 --> 00:19:51,500
These are the two main characters alone.

361
00:19:51,900 --> 00:19:53,840
But they manage to carry the entire film!

362
00:19:53,840 --> 00:19:57,240
It's incredible how a B-movie producer such as yourself

363
00:19:57,240 --> 00:20:00,320
managed to write such a perfectly crafted, poignant story.

364
00:20:00,540 --> 00:20:01,800
Ah, I see, I see.

365
00:20:02,660 --> 00:20:05,620
So you think I'm nothing more than a B-movie producer?!

366
00:20:05,620 --> 00:20:06,460
I'm so sorry!

367
00:20:07,220 --> 00:20:09,340
But, uh, it's not just the story.

368
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:11,340
You go from the bustle of the city,

369
00:20:11,720 --> 00:20:14,340
then to the sweeping, majestic views of the Alps.

370
00:20:14,720 --> 00:20:16,060
It's so liberating.

371
00:20:16,060 --> 00:20:18,700
If I saw that on the big screen,

372
00:20:18,700 --> 00:20:21,020
it would take my breath away.

373
00:20:26,020 --> 00:20:28,380
One more question for you, Gene.

374
00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:29,820
Out of all the scenes,

375
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:31,320
which one did you like the best?

376
00:20:31,820 --> 00:20:32,720
Uh, easy.

377
00:20:33,660 --> 00:20:35,320
It's the one where Lily is singing

378
00:20:35,320 --> 00:20:36,520
and then looks over her shoulder.

379
00:20:36,920 --> 00:20:38,580
Oh, that was a quick answer.

380
00:20:39,040 --> 00:20:40,660
Well, it's the moment when Delbert

381
00:20:40,660 --> 00:20:42,580
finally regains his passion for music.

382
00:20:43,080 --> 00:20:44,340
And also, when I read it,

383
00:20:44,680 --> 00:20:46,440
man, I could picture it immediately.

384
00:20:47,840 --> 00:20:48,320
Hmm.

385
00:20:48,780 --> 00:20:50,080
You have passed.

386
00:20:50,640 --> 00:20:52,160
However, looking through it,

387
00:20:52,520 --> 00:20:53,480
most of these scenes

388
00:20:53,480 --> 00:20:54,880
only have two actors in them,

389
00:20:55,140 --> 00:20:56,700
which means you're going to have to cast

390
00:20:56,700 --> 00:20:57,760
really strong actors.

391
00:20:57,960 --> 00:20:58,980
It's already cast.

392
00:20:59,320 --> 00:21:01,580
We offered Delbert to Martin Braddock,

393
00:21:01,660 --> 00:21:02,500
and he's accepted.

394
00:21:02,940 --> 00:21:03,100
What?

395
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:05,780
M-Martin Braddock?

396
00:21:06,780 --> 00:21:08,300
But he's a legend.

397
00:21:08,540 --> 00:21:09,660
The best actor ever.

398
00:21:10,620 --> 00:21:11,640
Uh, wait.

399
00:21:11,820 --> 00:21:12,520
Didn't he retire?

400
00:21:13,080 --> 00:21:14,760
He no longer felt inspired,

401
00:21:15,040 --> 00:21:16,320
so he got more picky

402
00:21:16,320 --> 00:21:17,900
and hasn't said yes to anything

403
00:21:17,900 --> 00:21:18,980
for ten years.

404
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:20,560
That is, until I got Grandpa

405
00:21:20,560 --> 00:21:21,400
to convince him.

406
00:21:21,640 --> 00:21:22,160
Wow.

407
00:21:22,640 --> 00:21:24,480
Martin Braddock's first film

408
00:21:24,480 --> 00:21:25,680
in ten years.

409
00:21:25,840 --> 00:21:27,140
That'll make for great PR.

410
00:21:27,500 --> 00:21:29,060
This movie's gonna be a hit now

411
00:21:29,060 --> 00:21:29,740
for sure.

412
00:21:30,220 --> 00:21:31,640
And for Lily we're casting.

413
00:21:32,080 --> 00:21:32,500
Um,

414
00:21:32,500 --> 00:21:33,720
excuse me?

415
00:21:33,720 --> 00:21:33,980
Yes.

416
00:21:34,240 --> 00:21:35,000
Right on cue.

417
00:21:36,400 --> 00:21:37,780
I got my hair cut

418
00:21:37,780 --> 00:21:39,020
just like you asked me to.

419
00:21:39,400 --> 00:21:40,100
How is it?

420
00:21:40,460 --> 00:21:41,480
Oh, nice.

421
00:21:41,920 --> 00:21:43,420
It really suits you, Natalie.

422
00:21:48,420 --> 00:21:48,940
It's...

423
00:21:48,940 --> 00:21:49,840
It's Lily.

424
00:21:50,160 --> 00:21:51,360
I'm staring right at Lily.

425
00:21:51,660 --> 00:21:52,180
I know.

426
00:21:53,400 --> 00:21:54,280
She's perfect

427
00:21:54,280 --> 00:21:55,860
because I wrote it for her.

428
00:21:55,960 --> 00:21:56,900
That's why...

429
00:21:56,900 --> 00:21:57,240
Oh.

430
00:21:58,180 --> 00:22:00,000
The first time I set eyes on her,

431
00:22:00,380 --> 00:22:01,680
I caught a glimmer of it.

432
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:03,620
A scene awash

433
00:22:03,620 --> 00:22:05,540
with a brilliant, beautiful light.

434
00:22:06,180 --> 00:22:06,740
And so,

435
00:22:07,100 --> 00:22:08,420
in order to try to capture

436
00:22:08,420 --> 00:22:09,800
that brief flash of light,

437
00:22:09,980 --> 00:22:11,000
I wrote this script.

438
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:13,420
So then sometimes,

439
00:22:14,020 --> 00:22:14,700
scripts are written

440
00:22:14,700 --> 00:22:15,820
for specific actors?

441
00:22:16,780 --> 00:22:17,300
Occasionally,

442
00:22:17,460 --> 00:22:18,480
an entire story

443
00:22:18,480 --> 00:22:19,560
will unfold in your mind

444
00:22:19,560 --> 00:22:20,140
when you meet

445
00:22:20,140 --> 00:22:21,080
that special someone.

446
00:22:21,580 --> 00:22:22,380
Generally speaking,

447
00:22:22,480 --> 00:22:23,160
that means you have

448
00:22:23,160 --> 00:22:24,320
a fantastic movie

449
00:22:24,320 --> 00:22:25,080
on your hands.

450
00:22:25,080 --> 00:22:26,060
I see.

451
00:22:26,420 --> 00:22:27,800
Um, excuse me,

452
00:22:27,880 --> 00:22:29,660
but can you explain to me

453
00:22:29,660 --> 00:22:31,440
what you two are talking about?

454
00:22:32,260 --> 00:22:33,520
I shall enlighten.

455
00:22:34,300 --> 00:22:35,820
Congratulations on being cast

456
00:22:35,820 --> 00:22:36,800
in your first movie.

457
00:22:36,960 --> 00:22:37,140
Huh?

458
00:22:37,840 --> 00:22:38,240
Ah!

459
00:22:39,100 --> 00:22:39,840
Wait!

460
00:22:40,020 --> 00:22:40,660
All of a sudden?

461
00:22:41,080 --> 00:22:42,440
How did this happen?

462
00:22:42,540 --> 00:22:43,240
Here you go.

463
00:22:43,340 --> 00:22:43,840
I don't believe it!

464
00:22:43,960 --> 00:22:45,280
I am going to let you

465
00:22:45,280 --> 00:22:46,580
shoot this screenplay of mine.

466
00:22:46,720 --> 00:22:47,540
Best of luck.

467
00:22:47,760 --> 00:22:48,980
Director Jean.

468
00:22:48,980 --> 00:23:03,340
I apologize,

469
00:23:03,620 --> 00:23:04,020
