1105
00:56:54,800 --> 00:57:02,280
What am I without music?

1106
00:57:03,380 --> 00:57:03,900
Nothing.

1107
00:57:08,220 --> 00:57:09,900
It's Mr. Peterson.

1108
00:57:17,840 --> 00:57:19,800
Uh, excuse me, sir.

1109
00:57:20,640 --> 00:57:21,260
Wow.

1110
00:57:24,800 --> 00:57:49,300
Oh, aren't you the...

1111
00:57:49,300 --> 00:57:49,860
It's Jean.

1112
00:57:50,220 --> 00:57:52,340
I, uh, didn't mean to disturb you.

1113
00:57:52,960 --> 00:57:54,160
But what are you doing?

1114
00:57:54,160 --> 00:57:56,580
I'm just having fun with some old film.

1115
00:57:59,000 --> 00:57:59,720
Amazing.

1116
00:58:00,380 --> 00:58:01,880
That's an actual film splicer.

1117
00:58:03,200 --> 00:58:05,180
It's an old man's hobby.

1118
00:58:05,880 --> 00:58:11,060
Trying to create a story from discarded film by piecing them together.

1119
00:58:12,060 --> 00:58:12,460
Huh.

1120
00:58:13,440 --> 00:58:13,760
Uh.

1121
00:58:14,820 --> 00:58:17,160
Um, I was wondering...

1122
00:58:17,160 --> 00:58:25,460
All the scenes feel important, and they're all wonderful performances.

1123
00:58:26,160 --> 00:58:28,600
But I have to cut a ton or it'll be too long.

1124
00:58:30,280 --> 00:58:31,100
Tell me.

1125
00:58:31,460 --> 00:58:33,000
Why don't you ask Pompo?

1126
00:58:33,000 --> 00:58:33,600
Uh.

1127
00:58:35,680 --> 00:58:36,000
Well...

1128
00:58:36,000 --> 00:58:38,080
I get it.

1129
00:58:38,420 --> 00:58:40,720
Pompo is the screenwriter, isn't she?

1130
00:58:42,880 --> 00:58:48,080
What's the point of you editing the movie if you can only do what she tells you to?

1131
00:58:48,420 --> 00:58:48,900
Right.

1132
00:58:48,900 --> 00:58:52,440
Jean.

1133
00:58:52,820 --> 00:58:53,100
Huh?

1134
00:58:53,820 --> 00:58:56,080
Who do we make movies for?

1135
00:58:57,160 --> 00:59:00,180
Well, for the audience, I guess.

1136
00:59:01,320 --> 00:59:05,440
And Jean, what is it about movies that you love so much?

1137
00:59:06,200 --> 00:59:06,560
Uh.

1138
00:59:06,560 --> 00:59:10,560
I'm guessing you found yourself in them.

1139
00:59:10,740 --> 00:59:12,020
Does that sound right?

1140
00:59:12,760 --> 00:59:17,380
Through them, you experienced empathy, dreams, longing.

1141
00:59:17,900 --> 00:59:19,640
You experienced life.

1142
00:59:19,640 --> 00:59:22,260
Now, tell me, Jean.

1143
00:59:23,220 --> 00:59:26,560
Have you found yourself in your movie yet?

1144
00:59:31,180 --> 00:59:31,860
Hmm.

1145
00:59:32,820 --> 00:59:36,020
Once you have, you'll find your passion.

1146
00:59:36,460 --> 00:59:39,020
Put it in your film, like you would an Aria.

1147
00:59:43,900 --> 00:59:45,460
What's my Aria?

1148
00:59:46,720 --> 00:59:47,160
What?

1149
00:59:47,440 --> 00:59:49,400
You've never strolled through the mountains before?

1150
00:59:49,640 --> 00:59:52,220
And how will doing that enhance my music?

1151
00:59:52,220 --> 00:59:52,560
Uh.

1152
00:59:53,260 --> 00:59:53,720
Hmm.

1153
00:59:56,600 --> 01:00:01,140
An Aria is a musical solo used to express emotion.

1154
01:00:01,460 --> 01:00:02,800
Back home, they worshiped me.

1155
01:00:03,360 --> 01:00:05,240
I devoted everything I had to music.

1156
01:00:06,980 --> 01:00:07,840
But for who?

1157
01:00:08,660 --> 01:00:08,840
Uh.

1158
01:00:10,220 --> 01:00:10,480
Ah.

1159
01:00:11,140 --> 01:00:11,480
Ah.

1160
01:00:12,600 --> 01:00:15,600
So ask yourself, what are you trying to express?

1161
01:00:15,820 --> 01:00:19,260
But also, to whom do you want to express it?

1162
01:00:19,640 --> 01:00:22,940
Do you even know how to read music?

1163
01:00:23,860 --> 01:00:25,140
Again, from the top.

1164
01:00:25,920 --> 01:00:26,700
What's wrong?

1165
01:00:26,820 --> 01:00:28,340
Do you think you're better than the score?

1166
01:00:28,480 --> 01:00:30,040
Is that why you're not following it?

1167
01:00:30,460 --> 01:00:30,820
No.

1168
01:00:31,200 --> 01:00:32,800
But if you want me to keep playing,

1169
01:00:33,460 --> 01:00:34,620
just answer me this.

1170
01:00:35,220 --> 01:00:36,840
What does this Aria mean to you?

1171
01:00:36,940 --> 01:00:37,100
Oh.

1172
01:00:39,680 --> 01:00:40,480
That's it.

1173
01:00:40,480 --> 01:00:47,720
I found it.

1174
01:00:47,720 --> 01:00:47,760
I found it.

1175
01:00:52,260 --> 01:00:53,960
Hold on.

1176
01:00:54,300 --> 01:00:55,120
That song.

1177
01:00:55,120 --> 01:01:08,260
Hold on.

1178
01:01:08,260 --> 01:01:09,800
I'm not on that one.

1179
01:01:09,800 --> 01:01:10,800
I love it.

1180
01:01:11,800 --> 01:01:12,800
Hold on.

1181
01:01:13,300 --> 01:01:13,620
Hold on.

1182
01:01:13,880 --> 01:01:14,980
Hold on.

1183
01:01:15,440 --> 01:01:16,260
Hold on.

1184
01:01:16,800 --> 01:01:18,220
Hold on.

1185
01:01:18,420 --> 01:01:18,600
I love it.

1186
01:01:19,820 --> 01:01:20,420
Hold on.

1187
01:01:20,740 --> 01:01:22,680
Hold on.

1188
01:01:22,680 --> 01:01:27,280
It's me. It's me.

1189
01:01:40,840 --> 01:01:45,280
Wait a minute. That means...

1190
01:01:45,280 --> 01:01:48,920
Do not fear, Port Pumple has returned!

1191
01:01:49,800 --> 01:01:51,260
I'm glad you're back.

1192
01:01:51,260 --> 01:01:53,640
Okay, Gene, I want to see the film.

1193
01:01:53,700 --> 01:01:55,300
Well, it isn't quite finished.

1194
01:01:56,960 --> 01:01:57,480
Huh?

1195
01:01:57,680 --> 01:01:58,840
It isn't quite finished.

1196
01:01:59,900 --> 01:02:01,080
Could you repeat that?

1197
01:02:01,240 --> 01:02:02,300
It isn't quite finished?

1198
01:02:02,460 --> 01:02:03,380
How long is it?

1199
01:02:03,460 --> 01:02:05,100
I started over from scratch,

1200
01:02:05,300 --> 01:02:08,060
and there's about 72 hours of footage left.

1201
01:02:11,340 --> 01:02:13,060
Well, explain.

1202
01:02:13,900 --> 01:02:16,960
Our very first screening is scheduled for two weeks from now,

1203
01:02:17,080 --> 01:02:18,800
and all of our sponsors will be there.

1204
01:02:18,800 --> 01:02:21,120
They're going to pull funding if it isn't ready.

1205
01:02:21,720 --> 01:02:25,420
So, you had better give me a damn good reason for this delay, Gene.

1206
01:02:25,460 --> 01:02:26,480
It's missing something!

1207
01:02:27,680 --> 01:02:29,960
A scene.

1208
01:02:32,260 --> 01:02:36,680
Are you telling me you want to do additional shooting?

1209
01:02:36,680 --> 01:02:38,520
Yes.

1210
01:02:40,460 --> 01:02:42,620
As I'm sure you're already aware,

1211
01:02:43,060 --> 01:02:46,160
additional shoots means reassembling the entire crew.

1212
01:02:46,800 --> 01:02:50,480
Now, Gene, do you have any idea how hard that is?

1213
01:02:50,940 --> 01:02:51,840
I can imagine.

1214
01:02:52,400 --> 01:02:54,200
You need a crew in order to make a movie,

1215
01:02:54,560 --> 01:02:57,260
but most of them have moved on to other projects by now,

1216
01:02:57,380 --> 01:03:00,580
which means calling in favors and rescheduling other shoots.

1217
01:03:00,580 --> 01:03:02,560
Who do you think pays for that, huh, Gene?

1218
01:03:03,000 --> 01:03:03,860
You do, miss.

1219
01:03:04,500 --> 01:03:07,460
And getting your cast to come back is an even bigger nightmare.

1220
01:03:07,960 --> 01:03:10,280
Who do you think negotiates that, huh, Gene?

1221
01:03:10,580 --> 01:03:11,540
You do, miss.

1222
01:03:11,720 --> 01:03:15,080
Lastly, who wrote the screenplay that you feel is missing a scene?

1223
01:03:15,200 --> 01:03:15,920
You did, miss.

1224
01:03:15,920 --> 01:03:17,320
Now I ask you again.

1225
01:03:18,040 --> 01:03:20,340
Are you telling me you want to do additional shooting?

1226
01:03:24,800 --> 01:03:26,380
Where is this coming from?

1227
01:03:26,460 --> 01:03:28,640
Because, quite frankly, I think it's insane.

1228
01:03:28,640 --> 01:03:31,740
You actually want to attempt that Arya again?

1229
01:03:38,480 --> 01:03:39,440
Don't there.

1230
01:03:39,700 --> 01:03:41,540
You're only embarrassing yourself.

1231
01:03:41,540 --> 01:03:42,780
I've never understood it.

1232
01:03:43,580 --> 01:03:45,580
No, I'd somehow forgotten it.

1233
01:03:46,200 --> 01:03:49,520
The smell of fish or the pain of slipping in the rain.

1234
01:03:50,100 --> 01:03:51,400
Even the breeding of goats.

1235
01:03:52,540 --> 01:03:53,020
What?

1236
01:03:53,260 --> 01:03:53,560
Stop.

1237
01:03:53,640 --> 01:03:54,780
Oceans, memories.

1238
01:03:55,140 --> 01:03:55,880
They're important.

1239
01:03:56,300 --> 01:03:58,220
And that young girl reminded me of that.

1240
01:03:59,100 --> 01:04:01,200
I know I can do the peace justice now.

1241
01:04:02,280 --> 01:04:02,960
Face it.

1242
01:04:03,100 --> 01:04:04,120
You're all washed up.

1243
01:04:04,600 --> 01:04:06,220
Why don't you just give up already?

1244
01:04:07,120 --> 01:04:08,840
Because in spite of everything.

1245
01:04:09,540 --> 01:04:10,740
This is all I have.

1246
01:04:10,740 --> 01:04:11,840
My art.

1247
01:04:12,280 --> 01:04:12,940
And so.

1248
01:04:13,360 --> 01:04:13,920
Please.

1249
01:04:14,400 --> 01:04:15,580
My music needs.

1250
01:04:15,660 --> 01:04:16,940
My movie needs.

1251
01:04:17,080 --> 01:04:18,380
Another shot at the Arya.

1252
01:04:18,560 --> 01:04:19,960
Another scene for the film.

1253
01:04:20,180 --> 01:04:20,900
I beg you.

1254
01:04:29,540 --> 01:04:30,180
Pombo?

1255
01:04:34,740 --> 01:04:35,340
Fine.

1256
01:04:35,780 --> 01:04:36,880
I'll see what I can do.

1257
01:04:36,880 --> 01:04:37,940
Huh?

1258
01:04:37,940 --> 01:04:38,440
Huh?

1259
01:04:43,060 --> 01:04:43,600
Unbelievable.

1260
01:04:44,120 --> 01:04:46,000
I never dreamed that kid would challenge me.

1261
01:04:46,600 --> 01:04:48,480
Why does he have to be so stubborn?

1262
01:04:49,160 --> 01:04:50,020
And yet?

1263
01:04:50,460 --> 01:04:51,960
I've never seen you happier.

1264
01:04:51,960 --> 01:05:05,060
Oh, Alan.

1265
01:05:05,300 --> 01:05:10,060
What do you want?

1266
01:05:10,060 --> 01:05:16,060
Well, sir, I've just been thinking that this job...

1267
01:05:16,060 --> 01:05:20,580
Wait, what is this thing?

1268
01:05:21,120 --> 01:05:22,740
Hey, who said you could take that?

1269
01:05:24,500 --> 01:05:26,840
It's an investment proposal for a film.

1270
01:05:27,400 --> 01:05:30,460
I was just about to call them to say that we aren't interested.

1271
01:05:31,040 --> 01:05:32,020
Wait, you're passing?

1272
01:05:32,740 --> 01:05:37,560
They canceled their screening because the director couldn't finish the edit in time.

1273
01:05:37,560 --> 01:05:40,160
So all their sponsors pulled out.

1274
01:05:41,220 --> 01:05:44,440
Investing in movies is a very high-risk venture.

1275
01:05:44,940 --> 01:05:47,520
What if the director never manages to complete the film?

1276
01:05:50,160 --> 01:05:53,980
Sir, give it to me, and I'll manage the account.

1277
01:05:54,920 --> 01:05:55,160
Huh?

1278
01:06:01,900 --> 01:06:02,540
Well?

1279
01:06:03,540 --> 01:06:04,680
It's perfect.

1280
01:06:04,680 --> 01:06:08,780
I never thought I'd have to write an additional scene at your request.

1281
01:06:09,440 --> 01:06:10,280
I'm so sorry.

1282
01:06:10,680 --> 01:06:13,740
I just thought it'd be more consistent if you were the one to write it.

1283
01:06:13,860 --> 01:06:17,780
I guess all directors end up as arrogant and selfish as you're being.

1284
01:06:18,960 --> 01:06:21,920
But who are you going to cast in this brand new part?

1285
01:06:22,160 --> 01:06:24,360
We don't have time to hold any auditions.

1286
01:06:24,640 --> 01:06:28,160
Well, actually, I already have someone perfect in mind.

1287
01:06:28,160 --> 01:06:33,660
The Meister script?

1288
01:06:34,380 --> 01:06:34,780
Uh-huh.

1289
01:06:35,020 --> 01:06:36,040
Yeah, that's right.

1290
01:06:36,220 --> 01:06:38,600
It's a new draft because they added one more scene.

1291
01:06:39,620 --> 01:06:40,060
Huh.

1292
01:06:40,760 --> 01:06:45,760
But our sponsors pulled out, so without additional funding, we won't be able to shoot it.

1293
01:06:46,560 --> 01:06:49,360
Actors are powerless in these kinds of situations.

1294
01:06:50,040 --> 01:06:50,340
Huh?

1295
01:06:50,340 --> 01:06:53,080
You can't have a board out there.

1296
01:06:53,560 --> 01:06:55,160
Allow me to just take this.

1297
01:06:56,340 --> 01:06:57,040
You're amusing.

1298
01:06:57,680 --> 01:06:58,660
Uh, uh, Mistia?

1299
01:06:59,200 --> 01:06:59,520
Hello?

1300
01:07:00,100 --> 01:07:01,520
Uh, oh, sorry.

1301
01:07:03,100 --> 01:07:03,720
Here.

1302
01:07:04,720 --> 01:07:05,060
Hmm?

1303
01:07:05,060 --> 01:07:11,680
Uh, this is awful.

1304
01:07:13,060 --> 01:07:15,060
How on earth am I going to market this thing?

1305
01:07:16,640 --> 01:07:18,360
Hello, Petersen Film.

1306
01:07:19,640 --> 01:07:22,460
Alan Gardner from the Alleywood Bank?

1307
01:07:23,100 --> 01:07:25,080
Your bank didn't get back to us.

1308
01:07:25,520 --> 01:07:27,560
I assumed they rejected our proposal.

1309
01:07:28,560 --> 01:07:30,400
Well, technically the bank has.

1310
01:07:30,780 --> 01:07:31,400
However...

1311
01:07:31,400 --> 01:07:48,900
You sure?

1312
01:07:50,360 --> 01:07:50,880
Yes.

1313
01:07:53,260 --> 01:07:54,120
That's good.

1314
01:07:55,120 --> 01:07:57,580
You prepared to lose everything if you fail?

1315
01:07:58,200 --> 01:07:58,720
Yes.

1316
01:08:01,400 --> 01:08:05,560
Then welcome to a world of dreams and madness.

1317
01:08:05,560 --> 01:08:25,420
This could get you fired.

1318
01:08:27,640 --> 01:08:30,020
There's no success without risk.

1319
01:08:30,760 --> 01:08:31,640
Am I right?

1320
01:08:31,640 --> 01:08:33,140
Yeah, but still.

1321
01:08:40,540 --> 01:08:42,300
Send me what you have and I'll help.

1322
01:08:42,400 --> 01:08:42,580
Huh?

1323
01:08:43,340 --> 01:08:45,400
You won't be ready in time at this rate.

1324
01:08:45,400 --> 01:09:01,440
And so, I recommend that we invest in Meister.

1325
01:09:03,080 --> 01:09:06,940
Even with a big star like Martin attached, the director's new.

1326
01:09:06,940 --> 01:09:12,260
It also doesn't bode well that the film's gotten bad press, which makes it too risky in my book.

1327
01:09:13,000 --> 01:09:14,320
I vote we don't invest.

1328
01:09:14,320 --> 01:09:19,820
The meeting's adjourned, then.

1329
01:09:21,820 --> 01:09:24,140
I haven't finished my presentation yet!

1330
01:09:25,140 --> 01:09:25,700
Mm-hmm.

1331
01:09:25,700 --> 01:09:32,480
I've always been good at embodying my characters.

1332
01:09:33,180 --> 01:09:34,600
Some say it's a gift.

1333
01:09:34,820 --> 01:09:35,120
Huh?

1334
01:09:36,560 --> 01:09:37,040
Yes.

1335
01:09:37,220 --> 01:09:39,020
It's been my dream since I was a girl.

1336
01:09:39,680 --> 01:09:42,900
I must have watched thousands of movies with my grandpa as a little kid.

1337
01:09:43,220 --> 01:09:43,960
But you know what?

1338
01:09:44,040 --> 01:09:46,420
Not a single one of them has ever truly moved me.

1339
01:09:46,420 --> 01:09:49,880
So then, is that what you're trying to do with this movie?

1340
01:09:50,360 --> 01:09:52,320
Except I can't be the one to make it.

1341
01:09:52,520 --> 01:09:54,640
Because then I wouldn't be able to enjoy it.

1342
01:09:54,840 --> 01:09:55,080
See?

1343
01:09:55,760 --> 01:09:57,160
That's where Gene comes in.

1344
01:09:57,560 --> 01:09:59,420
It's why you hired him to direct it.

1345
01:09:59,780 --> 01:10:02,800
Because out of all the movies, this one is your dream.

1346
01:10:03,680 --> 01:10:05,780
So how long has this been your dream?

1347
01:10:05,780 --> 01:10:08,780
Well, uh...

1348
01:10:08,780 --> 01:10:13,680
I've always loved watching movies, ever since I was a kid.

1349
01:10:15,860 --> 01:10:19,580
I didn't have any friends, but I didn't really care.

1350
01:10:20,540 --> 01:10:23,340
I mean, there were so many movies to watch.

1351
01:10:23,860 --> 01:10:25,340
So many I hadn't seen yet.

1352
01:10:27,300 --> 01:10:28,740
But there was a girl.

1353
01:10:30,040 --> 01:10:33,200
In every theater I went to, she was there.

1354
01:10:33,800 --> 01:10:34,940
She never stayed, though.

1355
01:10:35,780 --> 01:10:37,640
She always left when the end credits began.

1356
01:10:40,180 --> 01:10:42,020
I can't even remember her face.

1357
01:10:43,020 --> 01:10:44,780
But I always wondered...

1358
01:10:45,980 --> 01:10:48,120
What was it that drew her to the movies?

1359
01:10:49,760 --> 01:10:53,900
And had she never been so engrossed in a film that she wanted to stay till the end?

1360
01:10:54,640 --> 01:10:55,940
Until after the credits had rolled?

1361
01:11:00,740 --> 01:11:04,740
When I think about it, it's the reason I wanted to make a movie myself.

1362
01:11:05,780 --> 01:11:07,620
Movies saved me.

1363
01:11:08,840 --> 01:11:09,580
So maybe...

1364
01:11:10,080 --> 01:11:11,820
Someone else will be saved by mine.

1365
01:11:13,920 --> 01:11:15,340
And then I finally saw it.

1366
01:11:15,980 --> 01:11:17,100
Myself in the movie.

1367
01:11:18,860 --> 01:11:19,660
This movie.

1368
01:11:20,620 --> 01:11:22,780
I made it for the me all those years ago.

1369
01:11:24,180 --> 01:11:24,800
No.

1370
01:11:25,180 --> 01:11:26,400
Not just for me.

1371
01:11:26,400 --> 01:11:27,400
I see.

1372
01:11:27,400 --> 01:11:40,240
It needed a character that everyone can identify with.

1373
01:11:40,240 --> 01:11:43,580
Someone who, no matter who watches it, can believe in.

1374
01:11:43,580 --> 01:11:47,880
It's what helped me realize that the movie was lacking a very important scene.

1375
01:11:48,520 --> 01:11:52,260
One little crucial scene it required that would make the movie complete.

1376
01:11:53,860 --> 01:11:58,120
To show what we sacrifice in order to be able to pursue our dreams.

1377
01:11:58,120 --> 01:11:59,960
And so I beg you.

1378
01:11:59,960 --> 01:12:01,960
Please, make my dreams come true.

1379
01:12:04,960 --> 01:12:09,040
You know, things always came naturally, and I succeeded at everything.

1380
01:12:09,040 --> 01:12:10,040
Or so I thought.

1381
01:12:10,040 --> 01:12:13,840
But, looking back at it, what have I actually accomplished?

1382
01:12:13,840 --> 01:12:16,340
What's the point of anything if it doesn't have meaning?

1383
01:12:16,640 --> 01:12:21,780
And once I came to that realization, I knew I had to find something for myself.

1384
01:12:22,680 --> 01:12:27,380
I ask you, what is a banker's job really, if not to give people the means to fulfill their dreams?

1385
01:12:27,380 --> 01:12:34,180
So, please, we want to make a movie that will give people who have nothing a way to keep on dreaming!

1386
01:12:37,600 --> 01:12:39,720
I'll admit I agree with what you said.

1387
01:12:40,360 --> 01:12:42,760
That is exactly what bankers ought to do.

1388
01:12:43,220 --> 01:12:45,840
But we can't make decisions based on philosophy.

1389
01:12:46,100 --> 01:12:49,900
You need to convince us with concrete figures, not just nice stories.

1390
01:12:51,000 --> 01:12:51,940
Figures.

1391
01:12:57,380 --> 01:13:01,120
I have those figures.

1392
01:13:01,260 --> 01:13:01,720
You do?

1393
01:13:05,120 --> 01:13:06,020
What's this?

1394
01:13:06,720 --> 01:13:07,920
You've been filming us?

1395
01:13:07,920 --> 01:13:08,760
I do apologize.

1396
01:13:09,100 --> 01:13:12,600
But not only that, I've been streaming this entire meeting worldwide.

1397
01:13:13,160 --> 01:13:13,500
Hon.

1398
